        .init(id: "transport", title: "Build the gradient", stage: "Stage 4 · Electron transport chain", mnemonic: "Electron energy pumps H⁺.", duration: 8, playerID: "nad", accessibility: "Electrons from ten NADH and two FADH2 travel along membrane proteins. Their energy pumps hydrogen ions from the matrix to the intermembrane compartment. Oxygen accepts the final electrons and combines with hydrogen ions to form water.", bullets: ["**Where:** inner mitochondrial membrane in eukaryotes; **cell membrane** in prokaryotes.","Electrons from **10 NADH + 2 FADH₂** pass along electron transport proteins.","Their released energy pumps **H⁺ from the matrix into the intermembrane compartment**, building a steep concentration gradient.","**O₂ is the final electron acceptor.** It combines with electrons and H⁺ to form **H₂O**."]),
        .init(id: "chemiosmosis", title: "Let the protons flow", stage: "Stage 4 · Chemiosmosis", mnemonic: "H⁺ flows. ATP synthase goes.", duration: 7, playerID: "synthase", accessibility: "Hydrogen ions move from the intermembrane compartment down their concentration gradient into the matrix through ATP synthase. The enzyme uses this energy to make ATP from ADP and phosphate. Electron transport and chemiosmosis together yield up to thirty-four ATP in the theoretical model shown.", bullets: ["**H⁺ flows down its concentration gradient**, back into the **matrix** through **ATP synthase**.","ATP synthase uses the gradient’s energy to make **ATP from ADP + phosphate**: **chemiosmotic phosphorylation**.","The **electron transport chain + chemiosmosis** yield **up to 34 ATP** in this **theoretical model**."]),
        .init(id: "accounting", title: "Count the ATP", stage: "ATP accounting · per glucose", mnemonic: "Theoretical: 36. Actual: ~30.", duration: 8, playerID: "phosphorylation", accessibility: "The theoretical model shown uses three ATP per NADH and two per FADH2. Ten NADH and two FADH2 give thirty-four ATP. Add four direct ATP for thirty-eight; subtract a two-ATP shuttle cost for a thirty-six ATP theoretical maximum. Actual yield is approximately thirty ATP per glucose.", bullets: ["**Theoretical conversion rates:** **1 NADH ≈ 3 ATP**; **1 FADH₂ ≈ 2 ATP**.","**Direct ATP:** 2 from glycolysis + 2 from Krebs = **4 ATP**.","**Electron transport + chemiosmosis:** (10 NADH × 3) + (2 FADH₂ × 2) = **34 ATP**.","**38 − 2 = 36 ATP theoretical maximum** in this model. The **2 ATP deduction** represents the cost of shuttling cytosolic NADH’s electrons into mitochondria.","**Actual yield: ~30 ATP per glucose.**"]),
        .init(id: "efficiency", title: "Where energy goes", stage: "Real cells · actual yield", mnemonic: "Some ATP. Some heat.", duration: 6, playerID: "atp", accessibility: "Actual yield is approximately thirty ATP per glucose. Proton leakage and the transport of pyruvate and ADP into the matrix reduce the yield. About thirty-two percent of glucose's potential energy is captured in ATP; about sixty-eight percent is released as heat.", bullets: ["**Actual yield: ~30 ATP per glucose.** H⁺ can leak across the inner membrane, and transporting **pyruvate and ADP into the matrix** uses energy.","About **32%** of glucose’s potential energy is captured in **ATP**. The other **~68%** is released as **heat**."]),
